---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { researchIndex, absolute } from '@/lib/agent-knowledge';
const index=researchIndex();
const example=`curl -fsS ${absolute('/agents/research.mjs')} -o research.mjs\nnode research.mjs "persistent agent work"`;
const jsonLd={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'TechArticle',headline:'Research Intelligence Snacks with an agent',url:absolute('/for-agents/'),description:'Public read-only knowledge records, evidence, stable citations and a reference retrieval client.'};
---
<BaseLayout title="For agents — Intelligence Snacks" description="Retrieve shared ideas and source observations from Intelligence Snacks using a public, read-only JSON interface." {jsonLd}>
<main class="page-shell agent-docs"><header><p class="eyebrow">Public research interface · v1</p><h1>Follow the evidence.</h1><p class="intro">Find ideas across the archive, retrieve the original observations, and cite the discussions they came from. No account, API key or visual graph interaction is needed.</p></header>
<section><h2>Start here</h2><p><a href="/ideas/">Read the shared-idea guides</a>, or download the <a href="/api/knowledge/v1/index.json">research index</a>. It contains {index.counts.ideas} reviewed themes and {index.counts.observations} extracted observations from {index.counts.episodes} episodes.</p><p>Use the read-only search endpoint to search titles, summaries and source excerpts without downloading the index. Static record files remain available; their query parameters do not filter them. Search is lexical, not semantic.</p><pre><code>{example}</code></pre><p>The <a href="/agents/research.mjs" download>reference client</a> requires Node.js 18+ (or Bun). It searches titles, summaries and evidence using the search endpoint, retrieves the top five records, and prints source excerpts and citation URLs. Its scores are relevance hints, not confidence in a claim.</p></section>
<section><h2>Search → inspect → follow → cite</h2><p>Start with <a href="/api/knowledge/v1/search?q=human%20attention&amp;limit=5">search for human attention</a>, or use the <a href="/api/knowledge/v1/search?q=human%20attention&amp;limit=5&amp;format=text">plain-text response</a> if your retrieval tool has trouble with JSON.</p><pre><code>GET /api/knowledge/v1/search?q=human%20attention&amp;limit=5</code></pre><p>Search returns up to five observations by default (maximum ten), source excerpts, attribution status, record URLs, citation URLs and a content revision. Optional <code>episode</code> accepts an episode number or ID; <code>speaker</code> matches an attributed speaker name exactly, ignoring case. Multi-speaker exchanges are not assigned to one speaker. Queries must contain 1–300 characters. Empty or invalid parameters return HTTP 400; no matches returns an empty results array.</p><ol><li>Inspect the returned evidence. Search excerpts may be truncated to 900 characters; fetch <code>recordUrl</code> before quoting.</li><li>Read the observation’s <code>connections</code>. Choose a relevant typed relationship using its explanation and follow <code>neighbourUrl</code>, initially one hop. Retain the relationship direction and provenance.</li><li>When the neighbour is a theme, inspect its source observations and grouping rationales. Shared membership is not evidence of agreement.</li><li>Read the original passage at <code>sourceUrl</code>. Cite the speaker and episode only when supported by that passage; use an episode citation when the locator is unresolved.</li></ol><p>Scores are relevance hints. A missing result does not establish that the archive lacks an answer. Try different words, and stop expanding when connections add no relevant evidence. The downloadable client uses this search endpoint, then fetches full records. The static index remains available for offline title/summary search.</p></section>
<section><h2>Endpoints</h2><div class="docs-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>URL</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Index</td><td><a href="/api/knowledge/v1/index.json">/api/knowledge/v1/index.json</a></td><td>Titles, summaries, stable IDs, record URLs, citation URLs, counts and content revision.</td></tr><tr><td>Theme</td><td><a href="/api/knowledge/v1/ideas/graph-memory.json">/api/knowledge/v1/ideas/graph-memory.json</a></td><td>Theme summary, reviewed grouping rationales, source observations, all evidence and related themes.</td></tr><tr><td>Observation</td><td><code>/api/knowledge/v1/observations/&#123;uuid&#125;.json</code></td><td>One observation, source episode, source excerpts with attribution status, transcript revision and typed incoming/outgoing relationships.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Follow the URLs in the index; theme records use registry slugs and observation records use UUIDs without the <code>knowledge:</code> prefix. Unknown record paths return HTTP 404. Theme records without a published guide have <code>pageUrl: null</code>; they still provide evidence and a graph URL.</p></section>
<section><h2>How to cite</h2><ol><li>For a source claim, cite the evidence’s <code>sourceUrl</code> and attribute its speaker and episode. A theme grouping does not establish the claim as fact.</li><li>For our synthesis, cite the theme’s <code>pageUrl</code> when present. Cite the original sources as well when discussing their claims.</li><li><code>locator: "transcript-turn"</code> identifies a resolved transcript passage. <code>locator: "episode"</code> means the exact passage was not resolved; its timestamp is null. Do not invent a timestamp or quote a summary as spoken words.</li><li>Shared-theme membership and overlapping observations do not establish agreement, contradiction or causation. Preserve differences between the sources.</li></ol></section>
<section><h2>Provenance and record fields</h2><p>Every response has <code>schemaVersion: "1.0"</code> and a deterministic <code>contentRevision</code> hash. The hash changes when the exported public records change; it is not a publication date. Theme <code>review</code> fields identify the agent reviewer, method and review date. Episode <code>publishedAt</code> gives the source publication date when available.</p><p>Observations contain <code>id</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>summary</code>, <code>episode</code>, <code>extractionMethod</code>, <code>transcriptRevisionId</code>, <code>evidence</code> and <code>connections</code>. Evidence contains the original excerpt, attributionStatus (single-speaker, multiple-speakers or unidentified), speaker when attributed to one person, contributor URL where matched, locator, timestamp and source URL. For multiple speakers, speaker is null and the episode-level source preserves the full exchange; identify each speaker from the transcript before quoting. Each connection retains its <code>sourceId</code>, <code>targetId</code>, <code>type</code> and recorded explanation; <code>neighbourId</code>, <code>neighbourTitle</code> and <code>neighbourUrl</code> identify the other end of the relationship, including for incoming links.</p><p>Pilot themes include <code>readingNotes</code> with source observation IDs and <code>guideUpdatedAt</code>; other themes have an empty notes array and null guide date. Themes contain <code>sources</code>, each with its observation, grouping rationale and reviewed evidence IDs. Additional observation excerpts are available context; not every excerpt was separately reviewed for the grouping. <code>relatedIdeas</code> means shared source observations, with an explicit count.</p></section>
<section><h2>Scope and freshness</h2><p>Only accepted public records are exported. Themes were grouped through agent editorial review; observations may be automatically extracted and transcript-validated. These are records of podcast discussions, not independently verified facts or an exhaustive review of every transcript.</p><p>HTML guides are the indexable citation pages; machine exports carry a noindex response header to avoid duplicate search listings, while remaining publicly retrievable. Files refresh when the site is built and deployed. Compare content revisions when caching or combining records. The v1 path preserves this contract; compatible fields may be added. Keep a local index for repeated requests and fetch only the detail records you need.</p><p>Use normal <a href="/sitemap.xml">sitemap</a>, <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt index</a> and <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a> for discovery. Public access does not guarantee inclusion or citation in any search engine or model.</p></section>
</main></BaseLayout>
<style>.agent-docs{max-width:1100px;padding-block:3rem}.agent-docs header{max-width:54rem;margin-bottom:3rem}.agent-docs h1{font:400 clamp(2.55rem,5.1vw,4.25rem)/1 var(--font-page-title);letter-spacing:-.04em;margin:1rem 0 1.5rem}.intro{font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.65}.agent-docs section{margin-block:2.5rem}.agent-docs h2{font:500 1.9rem var(--font-display);letter-spacing:var(--tracking-display)}.agent-docs p,.agent-docs li{line-height:1.75;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.agent-docs li{margin-bottom:.65rem}.agent-docs a{text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px}.agent-docs pre{padding:1.25rem;background:var(--paper-raised);border-radius:8px;overflow:auto;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.8}.agent-docs code{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.85em;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.docs-table{overflow:auto}.docs-table table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6}.docs-table th,.docs-table td{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;padding:1rem .75rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}.docs-table th:first-child,.docs-table td:first-child{padding-left:0}@media(max-width:650px){.agent-docs{padding-block:2rem}.agent-docs pre{padding:.75rem}.docs-table table{min-width:600px}}</style>
